# Monitor State Persistence — Implementation Plan

## Problem

The multi-page dashboard architecture uses full-page Flask navigation. When the user navigates away from `/monitor`, the DOM, JavaScript state, and Socket.IO connection are destroyed. Returning to `/monitor` recreates the page in its initial empty state, losing the previous screenshot, answer, provenance, and pipeline logs.

If processing completes while the user is on another page (`/health`, `/usage`, `/history`), the `status_update` event arrives at the *other page's* Socket.IO connection, where the Monitor DOM elements don't exist — the result is silently lost.

## Proposed Changes

### Architecture Overview

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Socket.IO Events        →   Save to sessionStorage      │
│  (status_update,              (textline_monitor_state_v1) │
│   image_preview,                                         │
│   pipeline_log)                                          │
└──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┘
                           │
                           ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  /monitor loads  →  Read sessionStorage  →  Restore DOM  │
│                      (screenshot, answer, provenance,    │
│                       pipeline logs, active route, etc.) │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                           │
                           ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Navigation DURING processing:                           │
│  Events still arrive at the new page's Socket.IO.        │
│  socket_events.js persists state to sessionStorage       │
│  regardless of which DOM elements exist.                 │
│  On return to /monitor, latest state is restored.        │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

